Patch 10 -- identity by canonical offset, and a trap that names an unresolved GL slot.

TWO INDEPENDENT FIXES, BOTH FOUND ON 2026-08-19. Full record in
docs/briefs/MGS2_ISLAND_ENTRY34_FAULT_2026-08-19.md sections 14 and 18.

## 1. The marker is a witness, not an identity

`getAlternate()` scanned 64 bytes from EVERY branch and call target the dynarec
translated and published the first address whose window held a marker. The
published address does not have to be a function entry, and in production it was
not:

```text
entry 22 matched 0x7b919f23  = RVA 0x101b9f23, offset 0xe3 INSIDE the function
                               0x101b9e40..0x101b9f4d
id 22 marker at              RVA 0x101b9f59, in the NEXT function 0x101b9f50..
```

So `wined3d_rendertarget_view_invalidate_location`, an entry in the production
allow-list, was bound to a mid-function address in the function before it, 54
bytes ahead of its marker -- and because the first match publishes, its real
entry point could never route afterwards. Nothing observed is attributed to this;
it is a defect in the rule.

The rule is now:

```text
identity  =  the address at which this id's marker sits at ITS CANONICAL OFFSET
witness 1 =  the marker bytes themselves (unchanged)
witness 2 =  addr == module_base + canonical RVA, once the class-B resolver has
             established the base from two independent agreeing RVAs
```

The canonical offset and RVA come from `harness/island/full/gen_entry_identity.py`,
which reads them out of the MOUNTED DLL: markers from the byte pattern, function
boundaries from `.eh_frame` FDEs, because the shipped DLL is stripped. An id with
no canonical identity is not armed at all rather than armed on a guess.

Witness 2 is skipped, not failed, while the base is unknown: entries are matched
at translation time, which can precede the first class-B dispatch. That is why
the offset check carries the decision and the base check is confirmation.

Verified on the device: `MGS2 island: entry 22 matched 0x7b919f50` -- the function
start -- with all 18 armed entries still matching.

## 2. An unresolved GL slot now names itself

Production leaves an unresolved gl_ops slot NULL so a native call through it
faults at once instead of entering guest x86 bytes. That is right, but a fault at
address zero does not say WHICH slot, and entry 34's static closure had eight
candidates. `mgs2_island_gl_trap.S` provides one 8-byte stub per slot:

```asm
    movw    r0, #N
    b       mgs2_unresolved_gl_trap
```

`movw`, not `mov`: the ARM data-processing immediate is an 8-bit value rotated by
an even amount, so slot numbers like 3113 are not encodable and the assembler
would either refuse or grow the stub. `movw` takes a 16-bit immediate, so every
stub is exactly 8 bytes and the address of a stub is base + 8 * slot with no
pointer table. ARM mode, so the island's Thumb callers interwork through the
function pointer with bit 0 clear.

The handler prints the slot, its name -- from the island's own macro-generated
lists, through a weak `mgs2_island_gl_slot_name()` -- and the CALLER, taken from
`__builtin_return_address(0)`, which works because the stub arrives with `b` and
LR still holds the island caller's return address. Then it aborts: there is
nothing safe to return, and faking a GL call would change what the renderer draws.

It answered in one run:

```text
MGS2 island: called UNRESOLVED GL slot 90 (glDrawBuffer) from 0x62b61525
  -> wined3d_context_gl_apply_fbo_state+0x2a0, i.e. the inlined
     wined3d_context_gl_set_draw_buffer(context_gl, GL_NONE)
```

DIAGNOSTIC USE ONLY, which is narrower than "diagnostic build only" -- corrected
here rather than left as written. `mgs2_island_gl_trap()` returns NULL unless
MGS2_ISLAND_DIAGNOSTICS is set, so a production build hands out NULL and keeps the
fail-closed behaviour. The stub table and its handler are still LINKED, because a
.S file does not see CMAKE_C_FLAGS: 32 KB of unreferenced stubs plus one dead
string in a 27 MB binary. Gating the table itself would mean touching the global
ASM flags, which is not worth it for 32 KB. Neither fix has a frame-rate measurement and neither is
in a production binary.

